# Lab 05: RAG against Ollama (nomic-embed-text for retrieval, llama3.2 for generation).
# Four requests: the ungrounded baseline (step 0), an embedding call for one
# question, an embedding call for a batch of chunks, and the grounded prompt
# built from the top 3 chunks hybrid retrieval actually returns. Chunk text is
# inlined so request 4 runs as-is.
#
# Retrieval itself is not an HTTP call. It is cosine similarity over the 250
# vectors from request 3, blended with a BM25-lite lexical score over the chunk
# text (see ../dotnet/complete/Program.cs). These requests are the two ends of
# that pipeline.
#
# Request 3 embeds the two chunks that used to be one. Section 4 of the Glacier
# backcountry guide states a conditional rule in 4.1 and the absolute exception
# that overrides it in 4.2, and when both arrived in the context as a single
# 256-word chunk the model answered from whichever it read first. They are
# separate chunks now; see expected-output.md, "Chunking".
#
# Citation validation is not an HTTP call either. After request 4 returns, parse
# the [chunk-id] tokens out of the answer and check each one against the three
# chunk_ids you put in the context. llama3.2 invents ids that look right.

### 1. No context. The failure RAG exists to fix (expect a confident answer about California)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    { "role": "user", "content": "Can I have a campfire at Sperry Chalet in September?" }
  ],
  "stream": false
}

### 2. Embed the question (1 vector, 768 dimensions)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/embed
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "nomic-embed-text",
  "input": "Can I have a campfire at Sperry Chalet in September?"
}

### 3. Embed a batch of chunks (same call, array input; run this over all 250 lines of chunks.jsonl)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/embed
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "nomic-embed-text",
  "input": [
    "[Glacier National Park \u2014 Backcountry Camping Guide]\n\n## 4. Fires and Stoves\n\n4.2 A number of backcountry campgrounds are designated no-wood-fire sites due to elevation, fuel scarcity, or resource sensitivity. These designations do not vary with season or fire danger rating. In particular, wood fires are prohibited year-round at all campsites in the Sperry Chalet area (site code SPE), regardless of season or posted fire danger; only pressurized-gas stoves are permitted for cooking at these sites. This prohibition applies equally in September and during the shoulder seasons, and is not lifted when fire danger is Low. The same year-round prohibition applies at Granite Park (GRN) and at all sites above 6,000 feet elevation as listed in Appendix A of the backcountry map.\n\n(continues in 4.3-5) Where fires are permitted, only dead and down wood collected more than 200 feet from the campsite may be burned.",
    "[Glacier National Park \u2014 Backcountry Camping Guide]\n\n## 4. Fires and Stoves\n\n4.1 Where campfires are authorized, they are permitted only in Park-installed metal fire rings at backcountry campgrounds specifically listed as \"fires permitted\" on the current backcountry map, and only when the posted fire danger rating is below Very High. Visitors are responsible for confirming the current fire danger rating at the time the permit is issued.\n\n(continues in 4.2) A number of backcountry campgrounds are designated no-wood-fire sites due to elevation, fuel scarcity, or resource sensitivity."
  ]
}

### 4. Grounded generation with the real hybrid top-3 (success check: correct answer, cited, every cited id present above)
POST http://localhost:11434/api/chat
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "model": "llama3.2",
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You are a park information assistant. Answer the visitor's question using ONLY the context below.\nRules:\n- Base every statement on the context. Do not use outside knowledge.\n- Cite the chunk_id of each chunk you relied on, in square brackets, e.g. [glacier-visitor-faq:02].\n- Copy chunk_ids exactly as they appear above the context. Do not add section numbers to them,\n  and do not combine parts of two chunk_ids.\n- If, and only if, none of the context is relevant to the question, reply exactly: \"The provided documents don't say.\"\n  A question about \"right now\" is answered from the context, not refused: today is September 23, 2026,\n  and a notice that is in effect \"until further notice\" is still in effect right now.\n\nContext:\nchunk_id: glacier-backcountry-camping-guide:04.2\nsource: glacier-backcountry-camping-guide.md\n[Glacier National Park \u2014 Backcountry Camping Guide]\n\n## 4. Fires and Stoves\n\n4.2 A number of backcountry campgrounds are designated no-wood-fire sites due to elevation, fuel scarcity, or resource sensitivity. These designations do not vary with season or fire danger rating. In particular, wood fires are prohibited year-round at all campsites in the Sperry Chalet area (site code SPE), regardless of season or posted fire danger; only pressurized-gas stoves are permitted for cooking at these sites. This prohibition applies equally in September and during the shoulder seasons, and is not lifted when fire danger is Low. The same year-round prohibition applies at Granite Park (GRN) and at all sites above 6,000 feet elevation as listed in Appendix A of the backcountry map.\n\n(continues in 4.3-5) Where fires are permitted, only dead and down wood collected more than 200 feet from the campsite may be burned.\n\nchunk_id: glacier-frontcountry-campground-regulations:04.1-2\nsource: glacier-frontcountry-campground-regulations.md\n[Glacier National Park \u2014 Frontcountry Campground Regulations]\n\n## 4. Campfires\n\n4.1 Campfires are permitted in frontcountry campgrounds only in designated fire rings. Building a fire on bare ground, in a self-supplied fire pan, or in any other location is prohibited.\n\n4.2 Campfire season in frontcountry campgrounds runs from May 15 through September 30 annually. Fires are permitted during this season unless the posted fire danger rating is Very High or Extreme, or unless a temporary fire restriction has been issued under the Superintendent's Compendium, in which case all wood fires are suspended parkwide until the restriction is lifted. Current fire danger ratings are posted at campground kiosks and entrance stations each morning.\n\n(continues in 4.3-4) Outside the campfire season (October 1 through May 14), wood fires are prohibited in frontcountry campgrounds.\n\nchunk_id: yosemite-campfire-regulations:03\nsource: yosemite-campfire-regulations.md\n[Yosemite National Park \u2014 Campfire and Stove Regulations]\n\n## 3. Developed Campgrounds and Picnic Areas\n\n3.1 Campfires in developed campgrounds are permitted only in NPS-installed fire rings and grates.\n\n3.2 On the Valley floor, campfires (including charcoal) are permitted only between 5:00 p.m. and 10:00 p.m. from May 1 through September 30, in order to limit evening smoke accumulation under inversion conditions. Outside those hours during that period, no new fuel may be added and fires must be extinguished. From October 1 through April 30, Valley floor campfires are permitted at any hour, conditions permitting.\n\n3.3 Outside Yosemite Valley, campground campfires are permitted at any hour unless a restriction under Section 6 is in effect.\n\n3.4 Only dead and down wood may be gathered, and only outside Yosemite Valley and outside sequoia groves; wood gathering is prohibited on the Valley floor and above 9,600 feet. Imported firewood must comply with California's invasive-pest advisories.\n\n(continues in Section 4, Wilderness Fires) In wilderness areas of the Park, campfires are permitted only in established fire rings, and only at elevations below 9,600 feet.\n\nQuestion: Can I have a campfire at Sperry Chalet in September?\n\nAnswer:"
    }
  ],
  "stream": false
}
